WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.600
Time travelers and a giant penguin? That's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.212
scratching the surface of Jean-Claude&nbsp;
Van Damme’s greatest fights.

00:00:07.212 --> 00:00:13.680
In 1993's "Hard Target", Van Damme plays drifter&nbsp;
and former Force Recon Marine Chance Boudreaux.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:18.000
In the film, he stumbles upon a shadowy&nbsp;
enterprise that has the wealthy hunt homeless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.600
veterans for sport, headed by the villainous Emil&nbsp;
Fouchon. Determined to eliminate any loose ends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:28.000
Fouchon leads his men to pursue Natasha&nbsp;
Binder — the daughter of a murdered veteran.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:33.280
In one of Van Damme's most memorably bizarre&nbsp;
demonstrations of his martial arts prowess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:37.520
he uses his lightning-fast reflexes&nbsp;
to catch a rattlesnake lowering itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:41.840
behind an unaware Natasha. While many of&nbsp;
his best fights pit him against fellow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:46.480
martial artists and well-armed goons, his&nbsp;
scuffle with the rattlesnake captures the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:51.434
over-the-top sensibilities of "Hard Target"&nbsp;
and sets up the movie's explosive showdown.

00:00:51.434 --> 00:00:57.440
Of all of Van Damme's big screen team-ups,&nbsp;
perhaps the most unlikely of them all was with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:03.360
former NBA superstar Dennis Rodman in the 1997&nbsp;
action movie "Double Team." The film stars Van&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:08.800
Damme as counter-terrorism operative Jack Quinn,&nbsp;
who recruits arms dealer Yaz — played by Rodman.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:14.000
With his help, he sets out to take down terrorist&nbsp;
Stavros. After Stavros targets Quinn's family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.480
the action shifts to Rome as the unlikely&nbsp;
duo charges into the villain's trap.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:23.280
Following a chaotic gunfight in the streets,&nbsp;
Quinn enters a hotel where Stavros has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:27.200
keeping Quinn's pregnant wife. He faces&nbsp;
both a gunman concealing a machine gun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:32.000
in a large suitcase and a martial artist&nbsp;
wielding a switchblade between his toes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:36.800
"Double Team" is one of the most bonkers showcases&nbsp;
for Van Damme's action movie sensibilities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:42.172
and this Roman hotel scuffle exemplifies the&nbsp;
film's status as something of a guilty pleasure.

00:01:42.172 --> 00:01:48.160
The 1991 movie "Double Impact" has Van Damme pull&nbsp;
double duty as twin brothers Alex and Chad Wagner.

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:50.400
"Alex he's your brother!"

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:56.480
"Brother? Why? Because he looks&nbsp;
like me? I'm gonna change that…"

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:00.560
The twins were separated when their&nbsp;
parents were murdered by the Triads.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:04.640
Their mother was personally killed by&nbsp;
Triad hitman Moon, and Chad avenges his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:08.640
mother decades later. With Chad being the&nbsp;
less violent of the two Wagner brothers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:13.200
he quickly finds himself alone and punching&nbsp;
above his weight against the lethal Moon.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:17.920
The hitman immediately establishes his physical&nbsp;
dominance by throwing a fuel canister at Chad.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:22.560
This changes when Chad is able to leapfrog&nbsp;
over Moon as the latter attempts to charge him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:26.498
thus catching the killer off-guard&nbsp;
while our hero goes on the offensive.

00:02:26.498 --> 00:02:32.000
The 1995 action movie "Sudden Death" stars&nbsp;
Van Damme as disgraced fire marshal Darren&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:36.800
McCord. When a Stanley Cup game in the Pittsburgh&nbsp;
Civic Arena is targeted by terrorists pursuing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:40.640
the Vice President, it's up to McCord to&nbsp;
save the day while attending the game.

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:45.680
Of all the fights that he finds himself in, it's&nbsp;
the very first that's the strangest and most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:51.040
memorable of them all; our man goes up against&nbsp;
a terrorist disguised as the Penguins' mascot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:56.240
Iceburgh. While the sight of Van Damme squaring&nbsp;
off against the beloved ice hockey mascot is sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:00.842
to raise more than a few eyebrows, the fight&nbsp;
sequence itself is surprisingly effective.

00:03:00.842 --> 00:03:05.040
"Street Fighter" is admittedly one of the&nbsp;
more ludicrous video game adaptations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:09.920
but its climactic fight still belongs&nbsp;
on this list. Van Damme stars as Allied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:15.280
Nations commander Colonel William Guile, who&nbsp;
sets out to topple the fearsome dictator M.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:19.280
Bison before the tyrant can unleash his&nbsp;
genetically engineered super-soldiers.

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:23.760
Guile dominates Bison with his superior martial&nbsp;
arts skills until the villain activates his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:27.920
tech-equipped suit to gain the upper hand,&nbsp;
forcing our hero to rethink his strategy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:32.240
This final bout is filled with the kind of&nbsp;
campiness that makes "Street Fighter" watchable.

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:35.040
"You have made me a happy man."

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:36.560
"Next, I'll make you a dead one."

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:43.600
One Van Damme fight that lives on with&nbsp;
particularly entertaining notoriety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:47.760
is the bar brawl sequence from the&nbsp;
1989 martial arts movie "Kickboxer."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:51.920
The film has Van Damme portray amateur&nbsp;
kickboxer Kurt Sloane, who rigorously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:57.360
trains himself to avenge his brother Eric after&nbsp;
he is viciously maimed by Thai champion Tong Po.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:02.640
Honing his body and mind under the tutelage of&nbsp;
his mentor Xian Chow, Kurt has his first major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:07.680
test when his trainer arranges a group of surly&nbsp;
bar patrons to attack him while he's intoxicated.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:12.480
As tonally grim as "Kickboxer" can be at times,&nbsp;
the resulting bar fight provides much-needed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:17.600
levity while giving Van Damme an opportunity to&nbsp;
showcase his fighting skills. Drunkenly dancing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:21.600
and showing off just how limber his body&nbsp;
is, Kurt takes out the disgruntled patrons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:25.920
with relative ease. 26 years later, Conan&nbsp;
O'Brien would have Van Damme recreate the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:30.800
fight sequence in the midst of an interview,&nbsp;
cementing its prominence in the actor's career.

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:36.400
During the wonderfully absurd finale to "Double&nbsp;
Team," Stavros lures Quinn and Yaz into a Roman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:41.120
amphitheater. While Yaz is preoccupied with&nbsp;
the various gunmen lurking around, Quinn fights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:46.000
the main villain himself with his infant son&nbsp;
dangerously close to the explosive showdown.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:50.480
In addition to fighting a surprisingly&nbsp;
jacked Stavros, Quinn has to contend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:55.280
with a loose tiger around his son, all while&nbsp;
being careful not to accidentally trigger any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.280 --> 00:05:00.000
of the landmines set around the arena. These&nbsp;
prominent challenges make what could have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.560
a run of the mill battle into a genuinely&nbsp;
memorable action set piece, complete with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:09.120
a laughable product placement for Coca-Cola. As&nbsp;
gleefully over-the-top as the rest of the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:13.754
the ending of "Double Team" blows up the&nbsp;
stakes with a fittingly explosive spectacle.

00:05:13.754 --> 00:05:18.880
One of the darker entries in Van Damme's&nbsp;
filmography is the 2003 action movie "In Hell."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:24.240
He plays convict Kyle LeBlanc, who's sentenced to&nbsp;
life in a Russian prison after killing his wife's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:28.480
murderer. Kyle learns that the warden pits the&nbsp;
rival gangs against each other in fights to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:33.200
death while their jailers bet on the outcomes.&nbsp;
After being regularly harassed and threatened by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:37.760
his fellow inmates, he decides to enter the fights&nbsp;
for himself, beginning with his primary tormentor.

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:40.480
Van Damme's brawls throughout&nbsp;
"In Hell" are perhaps the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:44.560
graphically violent and grim of his career,&nbsp;
with the opening matches quickly setting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:48.720
the tone for the movie. While his movies have&nbsp;
always included their fair share of violence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:53.360
"In Hell" contains the goriest fight&nbsp;
sequences in his extensive catalog.

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:58.640
Van Damme plays slightly against type as&nbsp;
an antihero in the 1993 movie "Nowhere to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:03.040
Run." He portrays Sam Gillen, a robber&nbsp;
and escaped convict living out in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:06.880
wilderness. After befriending a single&nbsp;
mother named Clydie and her children,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:10.720
Sam faces off against a murderous real&nbsp;
estate developer named Franklin Hale.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:15.200
As he attempts to seize Clydie's land at&nbsp;
gunpoint, Sam arrives in the nick of time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:19.840
to stop this hostile takeover. A drag-out fight&nbsp;
follows with Hale's right hand man, Dunston.

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:25.360
Sam and Dunston's brawl is a dirty, raw skirmish,&nbsp;
and the fact that Van Damme's opponent isn't an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:28.880
equally chiseled tough guy makes the&nbsp;
fight sequence all the more visceral.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:33.600
As rough around the edges as the overall film&nbsp;
is, the climax to "Nowhere to Run" proves Van&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:39.280
Damme can deliver a fight that doesn't entirely&nbsp;
consist of impossibly graceful martial arts moves.

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:43.600
The 1990 film "Lionheart" has&nbsp;
Van Damme playing Lyon Gaultier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:48.000
a French legionnaire who deserts his unit&nbsp;
after his brother is murdered in Los Angeles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:52.160
To help the family recover, Lyon enters the&nbsp;
illicit world of underground fight clubs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:55.760
culminating into a showdown with&nbsp;
the undefeated bruiser Attila.

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:59.680
Attila is a hulking brawler who&nbsp;
takes the opening wave of blows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:05.120
tiring his opponent before brutally moving in for&nbsp;
the kill with particularly sadistic attacks. Lyon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:09.600
painfully discovers this strategy firsthand, but&nbsp;
the former legionnaire is able to turn the tables&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:14.720
on his large enemy. Among Van Damme's more brutal&nbsp;
fights, the battle in "Lionheart" gives the actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:20.320
the opportunity to get his hands dirty rather&nbsp;
than just effortlessly kick around his opponent.

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:23.120
In a rare antagonistic turn, Van Damme menaces his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:27.520
fellow action heroes in the 2012&nbsp;
blockbuster "The Expendables 2."

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:30.000
"Are you afraid of me?"

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:31.680
"No I'm not."

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:35.520
"You should be."

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:41.600
Portraying the aptly named Jean Vilain as a&nbsp;
rival mercenary leading his own private army,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:46.000
he ambushes the Expendables and ruthlessly&nbsp;
murders their youngest recruit Billy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:49.600
The seasoned soldiers of fortune&nbsp;
fight Vilain's forces across Europe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:54.160
leading to a climactic gunfight that erupts&nbsp;
in a Bulgarian airport. Expendables leader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:58.880
Barney Ross corners Vilain, resulting in a&nbsp;
hand-to-hand brawl between the combat veterans.

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:03.040
Van Damme clearly relishes the opportunity&nbsp;
to portray an outright villain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.040 --> 00:08:08.000
and as his duel with Barney escalates, he&nbsp;
more than proves he still has the moves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:12.640
For an excellent showcase of just how&nbsp;
effective Van Damme can be as a bad guy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:17.840
"The Expendables 2" picks up whenever he is&nbsp;
onscreen squaring off with the mercenary team.

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:22.880
Van Damme's most successful foray into&nbsp;
the science fiction genre is the 1994 film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:26.800
"Timecop," which is set in a world where time&nbsp;
travel has been made possible. To prevent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:30.720
crimes being committed across the timeline, the&nbsp;
government forms the Time Enforcement Commission,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:35.760
with Van Damme playing operative Max Walker.&nbsp;
After learning that Senator Aaron McComb is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:40.880
illegally using funds to finance his presidential&nbsp;
campaign, Walker is hunted by McComb's men.

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:45.520
In one of the film's earliest fights, Walker's&nbsp;
home is raided by assassins while he is sleeping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:49.600
on the couch. Our hero awakens just in&nbsp;
time to fight each of his would-be killers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:54.560
ending with Van Damme performing the splits as&nbsp;
his final enemy accidentally electrocutes himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:58.560
When it comes to protagonists in lone wolf&nbsp;
action flicks proving their worth by defending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:03.322
themselves from a home invasion, "Timecop"&nbsp;
is one of the movies that predated the trend.

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:07.600
Van Damme's most frequent co-star was&nbsp;
fellow action hero Dolph Lundgren.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:12.560
The two actors first collaborated in the&nbsp;
1992 sci-fi action movie "Universal Soldier."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:17.760
Van Damme and Lundgren star respectively as&nbsp;
U.S. Army soldiers Luc Deveraux and Andrew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:22.560
Scott — two men who killed each other in the&nbsp;
Vietnam War but are resurrected and augmented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:27.440
through genetic experimentation decades later.&nbsp;
As the revived combat veterans acclimate to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:31.920
their new lease on life, they begin to recall&nbsp;
their past lives and reignite their deadly feud.

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:36.400
It's clear from the outset that Van Damme and&nbsp;
Lundgren have an engaging onscreen rapport,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:39.520
and the climactic skirmish with the&nbsp;
two old enemies juiced on genetic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:44.560
muscle enhancers helped spawn an entire action&nbsp;
movie franchise. "Universal Soldier," however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:48.719
has the two action stars clash on&nbsp;
screen while they were in their prime.

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:54.400
1989's "Kickboxer," takes place in yet another&nbsp;
underground fight club — this one in Thailand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:58.400
Van Damme's character Kurt seeks revenge&nbsp;
for the brutal beating of his brother Eric.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.400 --> 00:10:02.560
Facing off against the villainous Tong Po&nbsp;
— the man who broke Eric's back — both Kurt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:06.320
and his opponent have their hands tied&nbsp;
up in roped gauntlets and are stuck with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:12.320
nasty shards of broken glass. Believing Eric to be&nbsp;
held captive by local crooks, Kurt is convinced he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:16.640
has to torturously go the distance to ensure&nbsp;
his brother's survival as he enters the ring.

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:20.640
Our hero endures the beating of his life —&nbsp;
saved by the bell at the end of each round&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:24.880
as he lets the fight draw out. Once Eric&nbsp;
reveals that he has escaped from captivity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:29.040
and arrives at the fight to support his&nbsp;
brother, a re-energized Kurt quickly puts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:33.322
the hurt on Tong Po in one of the more&nbsp;
brutal fights Van Damme has appeared in.

00:10:33.322 --> 00:10:38.494
Van Damme's breakthrough performance as American&nbsp;
soldier and martial artist Frank Dux in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.494 --> 00:10:43.760
the 1988 film "Bloodsport" remains a high point&nbsp;
in his career. Deserting from the U.S. Army to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:48.800
participate in a secret martial arts tournament&nbsp;
known as the Kumite, Frank takes on waves of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:53.200
opponents from around the world. Facing him at&nbsp;
the end of this high-stakes tournament is past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:59.200
champion Chong-Li, leading to a brutal brawl after&nbsp;
Chong-Li savagely beats Frank's friend Jackson.

00:10:59.200 --> 00:11:02.400
There's a lot going on in the&nbsp;
climactic bout of "Bloodsport"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:06.880
that makes it particularly memorable. Van&nbsp;
Damme's role in the film catapulted him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.880 --> 00:11:14.540
into cinemas worldwide as the&nbsp;
latest bona fide action hero.

00:11:16.787 --> 00:11:18.731
"That hurts me just looking at it."

00:11:19.360 --> 00:11:24.080
Much of Van Damme's career and fight sequences&nbsp;
would have to contend with the high bar set by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:27.840
"Bloodsport," and it remains the&nbsp;
gold standard in his filmography.

